1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:02,706
Mark Russinovich: Good afternoon, everybody.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:03,714
Ion Stoica: Good afternoon.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,130
Mark Russinovich: So it's good to see you all here at Build.

4
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:11,540
I'm Mark Russinovich.

5
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:13,170
Ion Stoica: And I am Ion Stoica.

6
00:00:13,204 --> 00:00:19,110
Mark Russinovich: And we've got kind of a fireside chat today, but it's a fireside chat where we don't have a moderator.

7
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:20,340
We're moderating ourselves.

8
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:27,360
So, we're going to be talking through a bunch of questions related to AI, AI infrastructure, models,

9
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:32,790
architectures for AI, and it should be a pretty engaging conversation.

10
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:33,270
Ion Stoica: I hope so.

11
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:39,960
Mark Russinovich: Yeah. So, I think we can start by talking a little bit of our background and why we're up here talking about these subjects.

12
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:49,650
So, Ion, you've got a background where you've had some amazing impact on the industry in data analytics, in AI, in your role as a professor,

13
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:56,310
in your role as a startup founder, Ray, vLLM, Databricks, Spark.

14
00:00:56,310 --> 00:00:59,880
Why don't you tell us a little bit about your background?

15
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:05,520
Ion Stoica: Yeah, so actually, starting around 15 years ago, at Berkeley,

16
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:08,740
we started to develop a little bit more,

17
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:17,880
quite a bit of systems, a few systems, initially in the space of domain of big data.

18
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:21,450
And started initially with Mesos and Spark.

19
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:26,880
Then after that, Ray, and more recently, vLLM and Golang.

20
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:35,160
And I think, as a high level, we're always looking about, obviously, trying to understand and to figure

21
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:42,600
out what is a problem you try to solve; and also, a little bit careful about the trends.

22
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:50,220
What I mean by that, a problem you have today, is that problem going to be bigger tomorrow than today?

23
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:51,475
And, you know like,

24
00:01:51,508 --> 00:02:02,220
things like, obviously, Spark was scaling up data analytics and making more interactive,

25
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:09,120
and much better support iterative kind of patterns like machine learning,

26
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:18,750
then Ray, complex scaling, complex AI workloads like reinforcement learning on heterogeneous hardware back then.

27
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:28,900
And, of course, vLLM is to us is clear that trying to exploit the power limits inherent of the GPUs.

28
00:02:28,934 --> 00:02:32,280
But then the memory became-- it was a bottleneck.

29
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:35,703
So, trying to alleviate that bottleneck and things like that.

30
00:02:35,737 --> 00:02:47,610
The one thing I want to say, because we are at the Microsoft conference here, is that I'm very happy that, actually, some of the things we've done has impact also

31
00:02:47,644 --> 00:02:51,420
with our kind of collaboration and partnerships.

32
00:02:51,420 --> 00:03:00,007
As you know, Spark and kind of Databricks, great, fantastic partnerships with Azure Databricks.

33
00:03:00,041 --> 00:03:08,409
And actually, today, we also announced a partnership between Azure and Anyscale, and Anyscale is around Ray.

34
00:03:08,443 --> 00:03:16,590
So, yes, very happy about that and very much looking forward to this partnership to grow.

35
00:03:16,590 --> 00:03:17,280
Mark Russinovich: Likewise, yeah.

36
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:18,510
It's been an awesome partnership.

37
00:03:18,544 --> 00:03:20,496
Ion Stoica: Yeah. So, you know,

38
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:32,610
on my side, you know, you you Mark, you have so much contributions, you know. You ship CC internals, then-- Mark Russinovich: Have you ever used these internals, Ion?

39
00:03:32,610 --> 00:03:33,899
I'm putting him on the spot.

40
00:03:33,933 --> 00:03:35,002
How many people have used it?

41
00:03:35,036 --> 00:03:42,495
Ion Stoica: But you architected Azure infrastructures, which I did use.

42
00:03:42,529 --> 00:03:51,402
And then you are now building and architecting AI infrastructure for Azure, and security are leading.

43
00:03:51,436 --> 00:03:58,813
So, one question I have, you've seen all these kind of phases, and you've been in distributed systems for a long time and security.

44
00:03:58,847 --> 00:04:02,910
So, what is new and what is old?

45
00:04:02,944 --> 00:04:07,714
What still applies and why is no longer applying?

46
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:09,622
Mark Russinovich: Yeah, well, that's a good question.

47
00:04:09,656 --> 00:04:19,672
I think one of the things that still holds, and this holds, I think, you'll even say for infrastructure and systems, is the fundamentals are still in place.

48
00:04:19,706 --> 00:04:27,010
I think if you take a look at fundamentally what's changed, it's these non-deterministic systems that are now carrying out workflows that they create on the fly.

49
00:04:27,044 --> 00:04:35,907
And that introduces a bunch of new risk area. You know, we -- In some sense, humans are non-deterministic agents creating workflows on the fly.

50
00:04:35,941 --> 00:04:38,008
But humans you can hold accountable.

51
00:04:38,042 --> 00:04:44,370
Also, humans, I think in general, are more reliable than the AI systems we have today, and certainly,

52
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:49,380
when it comes to humans, we designed our systems around human speed and scale.

53
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:56,730
And when you have agents now that are intelligent running around, they can perform lots of damage in a very short period of time.

54
00:04:56,730 --> 00:05:04,050
So I think if we get into it a little bit later, we'll talk about some of the things like prompt injection risks around that,

55
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:13,260
risks around jailbreaking, risks around hallucination; all of those things can cause workflows to go awry and for the system to not behave as you intend.

56
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:18,060
Ion Stoica: Yeah, and what about this kind of-- and you mentioned about the fundamentals still remain,

57
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:23,520
but again, to be more precise about concepts like consensus,

58
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:28,470
consistency, this kind of distributed scheduling,

59
00:05:28,470 --> 00:05:37,505
which of these concepts kind of remain more or less the same, and which are changing and morphing in support to support new orbit.

60
00:05:37,539 --> 00:05:46,140
Mark Russinovich: Yeah. Well, and I think you know we talk about AI and running these agent workloads, and you talk about the LLMs and hosting the LLMs.

61
00:05:46,140 --> 00:05:53,340
The things like the consensus around the infrastructure, knowing what is available and online.

62
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:55,200
Things like load balancing still matters.

63
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:56,820
Locality matters still.

64
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:01,814
So you want to route requests, especially when you talk about KV caches, which is the context.

65
00:06:01,848 --> 00:06:04,860
You need to make sure that the requests are going back to the same systems.

66
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:09,540
Otherwise, you're going to be wasting compute and bandwidth as you're moving things around.

67
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:11,700
So all of those things still apply.

68
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:17,010
I think, and this is an interesting question, I think, for you.

69
00:06:17,010 --> 00:06:18,390
Are these systems stateless?

70
00:06:18,390 --> 00:06:21,250
Do you consider them stateless or stateful?

71
00:06:23,430 --> 00:06:26,117
Ion Stoica: I think it very much depends.

72
00:06:26,151 --> 00:06:35,670
Certainly, because I will also, I win as a serverless trend.

73
00:06:35,670 --> 00:06:42,270
I think that quite a few systems become more stateful.

74
00:06:42,270 --> 00:06:55,140
That is because just the trend of bringing the state in and saving the state if needed for the next operation, in some cases, it's too high.

75
00:06:55,140 --> 00:07:00,810
And it's not only the state, leading the state and saving the state, it's initializing, right?

76
00:07:00,810 --> 00:07:07,350
If you are talking about initializing the GPUs and so forth, it can take a long time.

77
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:12,060
So this forces you to be marked more stateful.

78
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:16,680
The problem with that is that it complicates, like we all know, the fault tolerance.

79
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:17,950
Mark Russinovich: Yeah.

80
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:27,240
So when it comes to these distributed infrastructures for AI, which include the GPUs, high bandwidth interconnections,

81
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:35,760
the KV cache, when you look at distributed systems through its evolution, what assumptions change

82
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:39,100
with an architecture to support this kind of computation?

83
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:44,970
Ion Stoica: So, I think it very much --great question -- very much depends on where you are coming from.

84
00:07:44,970 --> 00:07:50,550
So you're coming from Spark, it's like big data.

85
00:07:50,550 --> 00:07:53,670
It's about, it's like, MapReduce plus plus.

86
00:07:53,670 --> 00:08:00,750
It's bulk synchronous processing, which is a model, which is a pretty simple model.

87
00:08:00,750 --> 00:08:02,400
You partition the data.

88
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:03,960
You have stages at every stage.

89
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:10,140
You have applied the same task on different partitions and do a shuffle between them.

90
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:20,190
What happens with, for instance, you talk about KV cache and inference, things are becoming so much more coupled, tightly coupled.

91
00:08:20,190 --> 00:08:33,840
If you think about mixture of experts and then you have the KV cache and you need to distribute it across different GPUs on the same node and even across nodes,

92
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:43,618
then it's things are becoming-- everything becoming much more coupled and more difficult because of that.

93
00:08:43,652 --> 00:08:49,418
Now, if you come from maybe HPC, maybe you can say, "I told you so."

94
00:08:49,452 --> 00:08:50,190
Mark Russinovich: Yeah.

95
00:08:50,190 --> 00:08:58,889
Ion Stoica: But even there I think that with HPC, it was much more -- you don't have such a . . .

96
00:08:59,295 --> 00:09:03,896
tightly coupling between the data and, kind of. . .

97
00:09:04,994 --> 00:09:08,760
-- and the compute.

98
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:17,070
Because even there, the data was -- you know, you don't have actually-- in traditional HPC, you don't have a lot of data to start with.

99
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:19,080
Because you do simulations, right?

100
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:20,160
And things like that, right?

101
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:22,912
This was kind of the workload.

102
00:09:22,946 --> 00:09:24,885
So I think that's kind of what it is.

103
00:09:24,919 --> 00:09:28,319
And this creates a lot of challenges.

104
00:09:28,353 --> 00:09:40,102
And since even you're talking about load balancing, things are changing so much more quickly if you think about expert parallel load balancing.

105
00:09:40,136 --> 00:09:48,301
Because you can change from layer to layer when you go in the forward pass to do the inference to do serving.

106
00:09:48,335 --> 00:09:56,655
And the other thing what happens, I'll probably talk more about that, is that things are evolving so quickly, right?

107
00:09:56,689 --> 00:09:59,820
By the time you build something, you have new requirements. It's never ending.

108
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:01,090
Mark Russinovich: Yeah.

109
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:04,370
Ion Stoica: Yeah.

110
00:10:04,370 --> 00:10:08,960
So, we have this kind of, I remember like it was 15 years,

111
00:10:08,960 --> 00:10:19,500
10, 15 years ago when people started the cloud computing and so forth, and people are saying the data center is a new computer.

112
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:32,940
I'm just curious right now, is that kind of abstraction still of actuality because now you can have training spanning different

113
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:35,790
availability zones and things like that.

114
00:10:35,790 --> 00:10:37,110
I'm curious about that.

115
00:10:37,110 --> 00:10:41,190
Mark Russinovich: I mean, I think if you take a look when that statement was made,

116
00:10:41,190 --> 00:10:47,220
it was when a data center would hold all the compute that you could imagine in the data centers at that time.

117
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:57,320
Even if you take a look at the evolution of Azure as a hyperscaler, the availability zone kind of became a unit of compute, but even in an availability zone,

118
00:10:57,320 --> 00:10:58,790
we have multiple data centers typically.

119
00:10:58,790 --> 00:11:01,940
It's not just one data center per an availability zone.

120
00:11:01,940 --> 00:11:04,950
Then you have a region, you have multiple availability zones.

121
00:11:04,950 --> 00:11:12,210
So even for traditional IT workloads or serverless computing, your application can be spread across many data centers.

122
00:11:12,210 --> 00:11:20,730
And now, you mentioned distributed training and AI training, and that's where you run into these AI super factories as we call them,

123
00:11:20,730 --> 00:11:26,940
where you've got massive datacenters just filled with GPUs specifically to host that training workload.

124
00:11:26,940 --> 00:11:38,220
And for that, we passed the line several years ago of a single datacenter being enough to hold the scale of the AI supercomputers that we're building now to train these frontier models.

125
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:47,850
And then over the last couple of years, we've crossed a line of even one region of datacenters is not big enough to train these models.

126
00:11:47,850 --> 00:11:54,720
And now we have these Fairwater systems that we've launched these AI super factories where there are multiple Fairwaters in Wisconsin,

127
00:11:54,720 --> 00:12:05,490
multiple in Atlanta, and they're connected with a dedicated network so that you can have a job that spans the GPUs across both regions with a very direct cable

128
00:12:05,490 --> 00:12:10,230
between them doing RDMA across the two regions.

129
00:12:10,230 --> 00:12:11,580
Ion Stoica: Yeah, it's fascinating.

130
00:12:11,580 --> 00:12:18,210
Now, going back again, like we discussed a little bit about serverless, right? The serverless-- Mark Russinovich: My favorite services.

131
00:12:18,210 --> 00:12:22,579
Ion Stoica: Yeah. So, what what does it mean in. . .

132
00:12:22,612 --> 00:12:32,790
this kind of-- when you have today's long-lived agents, which can go hours, sometimes days?

133
00:12:32,790 --> 00:12:40,800
Mark Russinovich: Well, if you talk about serverless, it's kind of people speak about it synonymously with containers.

134
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:42,960
Serverless equals containers.

135
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:47,970
And when people think containers, they think the original serverless containers,

136
00:12:47,970 --> 00:12:51,840
which were like Lambda or Azure Functions,

137
00:12:51,840 --> 00:13:00,330
which are short-lived, and so people conflate serverless with short-lived applications or pieces of code,

138
00:13:00,330 --> 00:13:05,580
when in fact serverless doesn't have an opinion about what code runs in these containers,

139
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:10,810
and so you could put SQL Server inside of a container, and it can run for years.

140
00:13:11,850 --> 00:13:13,230
And it also can be monstrous.

141
00:13:13,230 --> 00:13:15,090
It can have terabytes of memory.

142
00:13:15,090 --> 00:13:18,660
So I think for me, I separate the two.

143
00:13:18,660 --> 00:13:23,250
And I say serverless is you don't worry about the infrastructure, it is containerized for the most part,

144
00:13:23,250 --> 00:13:30,270
although we talk about lightweight containers now and Wasm sandboxes which are also a form of serverless.

145
00:13:30,270 --> 00:13:38,700
But that's where I think the whole trend-- if you take a look at Azure's architectural evolution, we're moving everything to serverless.

146
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:50,590
We're moving the whole infrastructure to containers that are on a serverless infrastructure that is doing the scheduling and placement and for the job, which is expressing constraints.

147
00:13:52,950 --> 00:13:56,100
Ion Stoica: So now continuing on that note.

148
00:13:56,100 --> 00:14:07,920
We have our infra, we build these infrastructures mostly to serve requests initiated by people directly or via BI tools and things like that.

149
00:14:07,920 --> 00:14:19,920
But now we are moving rapidly to an age of agents, to having agent loops instead of just requests as a unit of work.

150
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:25,860
How does this impact, do you think, about architecture stack?

151
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:33,480
Mark Russinovich: Well, I think we're still at the early days of this, actually, because just in the last few years, we went from chat

152
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:42,060
to now agentic systems, which are this loop where the agents are talking to other systems as part of their computation.

153
00:14:42,060 --> 00:14:43,830
And they might even be talking to other agents.

154
00:14:43,830 --> 00:14:56,610
And so when it comes to how you define an agentic application that consists of these things, I think we're still in the early days of specifying the application model for these.

155
00:14:56,610 --> 00:14:58,380
And a lot of it is, I think, still ad hoc.

156
00:14:58,380 --> 00:15:01,980
But we're getting towards what is an agentic application?

157
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:05,940
What are the components? How do you place them for efficiency?

158
00:15:05,940 --> 00:15:11,550
How do you automatically deploy them instead of taking piecemeal and deploying and connecting them together after the fact?

159
00:15:11,550 --> 00:15:13,860
So in my opinion, I think that we're still early.

160
00:15:13,860 --> 00:15:16,720
But I'd be curious to hear about what you think?

161
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:21,960
Ion Stoica: So, yeah, I think we are early.

162
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:26,850
The one thing I see is that constant increase in complexity.

163
00:15:26,850 --> 00:15:27,540
Mark Russinovich: That's for sure.

164
00:15:27,540 --> 00:15:32,850
Ion Stoica: Even the definition of agents, what is an agent?

165
00:15:32,850 --> 00:15:36,570
Well, was an agent an LLM invoking a tool?

166
00:15:36,570 --> 00:15:39,000
But now it's much more than that.

167
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,570
You have a tool, you have an entire harnessing, right?

168
00:15:42,570 --> 00:15:44,820
Which can be very complex, right?

169
00:15:44,820 --> 00:15:52,140
So now, and then you are talking about some kind of continual learning.

170
00:15:52,140 --> 00:15:58,890
People talk more and more whether this happens in context, continuously evolving the prompt.

171
00:15:58,890 --> 00:16:02,490
And in some cases even updating the weights, right?

172
00:16:02,490 --> 00:16:05,490
So kind of like RL.

173
00:16:05,490 --> 00:16:16,179
So, and then you have this kind of, you need something to support very diverse and complex. . .

174
00:16:16,212 --> 00:16:19,110
you know, application.

175
00:16:19,110 --> 00:16:22,620
You need to do, for sure, kind of the inference,

176
00:16:22,620 --> 00:16:31,620
then basically arbitrary application-- running arbitrary application because that's part of the harnessing, right?

177
00:16:31,620 --> 00:16:38,730
And then maybe you are going also to do some kind of training if you want to update the policy.

178
00:16:38,730 --> 00:16:40,410
And so that's kind of things.

179
00:16:40,410 --> 00:16:45,660
And when you have the models, of course, you can have the models which is going to be co-located.

180
00:16:45,660 --> 00:16:50,490
It has to be co-located because if you want to update it,

181
00:16:50,490 --> 00:16:55,050
or it just can be something running outside,

182
00:16:55,050 --> 00:16:59,790
it can be like OpenAI or like a new model that's released by Microsoft.

183
00:16:59,790 --> 00:17:02,820
So that's kind of, it's so complex.

184
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:08,720
So you need to think-- to have something which is very flexible to support these workloads.

185
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:11,360
And yeah, I think that we'll see.

186
00:17:11,360 --> 00:17:16,460
I assume that in time, things will stabilize one way or another, but we are very early.

187
00:17:16,460 --> 00:17:22,579
Mark Russinovich: Yeah. So one of the things that I'd be really curious to hear your thoughts on,

188
00:17:22,612 --> 00:17:25,920
in the systems that we've designed, they've largely been deterministic.

189
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:29,160
You design the system, you've got a workload, you optimize for it.

190
00:17:29,160 --> 00:17:31,920
These systems are inherently non-deterministic, as we talked about.

191
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:32,430
Ion Stoica: Yep.

192
00:17:32,430 --> 00:17:36,160
Mark Russinovich: And so how does that change the way you design a system?

193
00:17:38,250 --> 00:17:41,370
Ion Stoica: That's a great question.

194
00:17:41,370 --> 00:17:48,120
And just to be clear, it's one of the reasons they are non-deterministic because, obviously, the LLMs themselves are non-deterministic.

195
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:53,880
If you send the same prompt over and over again, you shouldn't expect, or you are not going to expect the same results.

196
00:17:53,880 --> 00:18:02,220
Actually, in some cases, you rely on that to get diversity in order to get better solutions.

197
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:08,190
So I think it's like there are a few things to consider.

198
00:18:08,190 --> 00:18:13,740
One, first of all, what eventually, what is your output?

199
00:18:13,740 --> 00:18:21,890
And in some cases, like with coding and so forth, the artifact can be still deterministic, right?

200
00:18:21,890 --> 00:18:29,420
You use this to create, to optimize the system, to synthesize even a system, and that one kind of is deterministic.

201
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:36,870
You can have harnessing tests and so forth to check, and then you can have metrics to high hill-climb.

202
00:18:36,870 --> 00:18:39,810
If you want to optimize, optimize for throughput, for latency.

203
00:18:39,810 --> 00:18:43,080
So the output is still kind of deterministic.

204
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:50,900
I think now this doesn't answer the question about how you debug your kind of evolution systems or agentic system.

205
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:55,430
And unfortunately, there, it's like, you have to presumably,

206
00:18:55,430 --> 00:19:04,640
and what you do is, record the inputs and outputs and the traces and then try to replace the traces, right, to find what the problem is if there is a problem.

207
00:19:04,640 --> 00:19:05,940
Mark Russinovich: Yeah.

208
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:19,830
So when you talk about an AI stack, you talk about GPUs, network, you talk about different types of accelerators.

209
00:19:19,830 --> 00:19:25,800
Where do you see the optimizations happening, the most important ones?

210
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:31,620
Or have we reached some limit someplace where there's the bottleneck that we need to focus on?

211
00:19:31,620 --> 00:19:34,860
Ion Stoica: I think clearly it will happen.

212
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:40,190
I think there are three. . .

213
00:19:40,223 --> 00:19:43,560
levels of optimizations will happen.

214
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:46,890
At the top level, there will be many algorithmic optimizations still.

215
00:19:46,890 --> 00:19:55,290
So I assume that a few orders of magnitude improvement over the next few years will come from the algorithms.

216
00:19:55,290 --> 00:20:03,960
What I mean by algorithms, if you look like, for instance, just inference or training, right now, of course, the workload changes, you have agents.

217
00:20:03,960 --> 00:20:08,550
You have much larger prompts or, of course, outputs.

218
00:20:08,550 --> 00:20:11,280
The context, again, is much larger.

219
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:13,800
You see a lot of optimizations.

220
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:21,330
And architectural optimizations, software architecture, like, for instance, sparse attention, different kinds of attention.

221
00:20:21,330 --> 00:20:24,000
You see different layers, different attentions.

222
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:27,090
And now you see even within the same layer different attentions.

223
00:20:27,090 --> 00:20:31,110
Even maybe you'll have per-token learned attentions and things like that.

224
00:20:31,110 --> 00:20:34,890
So that's kind of what can give you a big improvement.

225
00:20:34,890 --> 00:20:38,760
Of course, this comes with a lot of complexity.

226
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:51,590
Then, obviously, you are going to have GPUs, the accelerators, which are going to still make progress, 1.5 or 1.6 every year improvements.

227
00:20:51,590 --> 00:20:57,950
And that's coming, again, with complexity because it's not like Moore's law used to be, right?

228
00:20:57,950 --> 00:21:02,420
The instructions that architecture is more or less the same.

229
00:21:02,420 --> 00:21:12,690
How you get these improvements, a lot of them is by adding new instructions which are targeting particularly new segments of the workload

230
00:21:12,690 --> 00:21:20,580
like I expect you are going to have more and more instructions in the GPUs to target this kind of sophisticated attentions.

231
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:26,100
And then, from the inherent, this will go a little bit slower.

232
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:35,330
You are still going to have smaller transistors; you are going to still be more power-efficient for the power per flops will improve and things like that.

233
00:21:35,330 --> 00:21:37,550
But these are multiplicative, right?

234
00:21:37,550 --> 00:21:45,210
So when you multiply them, you can get massive gains in the next few years.

235
00:21:45,210 --> 00:21:47,370
Mark Russinovich: Okay, I have another question for you.

236
00:21:47,370 --> 00:21:56,850
Because you've been involved so much with open source in AI infrastructure and analytics, do you see a canonical open source-based stack?

237
00:21:56,850 --> 00:22:01,206
And do you think it will converge or there will be diversity?

238
00:22:03,099 --> 00:22:11,580
Ion Stoica: I think that there is some stack which is used by companies.

239
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:20,970
There is like-- and, you see-- you have at the lower level Kubernetes or Slurm.

240
00:22:20,970 --> 00:22:31,500
Under the hood, if you want to move, because everyone now has to want to have the freedom to make a move across regions or from region to region, maybe you have something like SkyPilot.

241
00:22:31,500 --> 00:22:39,840
On top of that, you have to have some framework like Ray or Pathways or something like that, which helps you

242
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:45,150
to distribute this kind of AI workloads; then obviously you have PyTorch and so forth.

243
00:22:45,150 --> 00:22:53,460
So I think you can put together this kind of stack, and then you have vLLM, or Golang, whatever, TensorRT-LLM as an inference layer.

244
00:22:53,460 --> 00:22:58,260
And then post-training, you have kind of WELL or SkyRL, and things like that, right?

245
00:22:58,260 --> 00:23:00,540
So you can create a stack, and you have a stack.

246
00:23:00,540 --> 00:23:02,070
I think a pretty good stack.

247
00:23:02,070 --> 00:23:14,280
The question is about what will happen and how this kind of stack will evolve because it's like the one thing

248
00:23:14,280 --> 00:23:24,750
which I see in terms of complexity increasing is that we used to build-- one of the things you teach students,

249
00:23:24,750 --> 00:23:29,880
right, and we learn ourselves that how do you build a complex system?

250
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:32,730
You build this kind of in a modular way, right?

251
00:23:32,730 --> 00:23:40,050
So you have these components with nice abstractions, clean abstractions which abstract away the implementation, right?

252
00:23:40,050 --> 00:23:45,180
So then you can evolve each of them independently.

253
00:23:45,180 --> 00:23:49,679
Now, the problem with that-- and this is when we are talking about these layers and all these components,

254
00:23:49,712 --> 00:23:54,810
the problem with that is that, as we know, it also comes with some overhead.

255
00:23:54,810 --> 00:24:03,930
And it used to be perfectly fine to say okay, we are going to spend a few percent, 10% overhead had to have this kind of nice available system, because at the end of the day,

256
00:24:03,930 --> 00:24:10,420
we can be even faster because we are going to evolve these systems, and the system is going to evolve quickly and quicker.

257
00:24:11,550 --> 00:24:18,210
But now it's like it's so much breaking the layering, and it's called cross-layer optimizations, right?

258
00:24:18,210 --> 00:24:22,560
It's like, this is what I see, everything, right?

259
00:24:22,560 --> 00:24:34,710
I don't know, it's like, in some sense, it's like all these layers are going to get crossed and broken.

260
00:24:34,710 --> 00:24:39,330
And I see, you can do maybe in an orderly way, but I don't know where you are stopping.

261
00:24:39,330 --> 00:24:43,530
Is this collaboration between Ray and Kubernetes, with you guys?

262
00:24:43,530 --> 00:24:54,720
But what is the thing here is that something like Kubernetes wants to know more information about the applications from Ray to make more intelligent decisions, something like Ray, which is up there.

263
00:24:54,720 --> 00:24:58,620
They want now to have more control to tell Kubernetes what to do. Right?

264
00:24:58,620 --> 00:25:00,600
So you can allow that.

265
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:07,500
But I don't know when you stop because, of course, you are no longer going to have these kind of clean, narrow abstractions.

266
00:25:07,500 --> 00:25:11,070
I think that's very interesting.

267
00:25:11,070 --> 00:25:14,670
Mark Russinovich: So, we've got about five minutes left, I think.

268
00:25:14,670 --> 00:25:18,534
Ion Stoica: Yeah, so. . .

269
00:25:18,576 --> 00:25:23,874
So, one question I do have for you,

270
00:25:24,992 --> 00:25:33,780
It's how do you think about-- we are talking about the workflows, agents, and so forth.

271
00:25:33,780 --> 00:25:41,250
Is that, you think about like a boundary, two different kind of patterns of workloads, or you think are--

272
00:25:41,250 --> 00:25:43,080
Mark Russinovich: For agents and-- Ion Stoica: And workflows.

273
00:25:43,080 --> 00:25:43,530
Mark Russinovich: And workflows.

274
00:25:43,530 --> 00:25:44,907
Ion Stoica: Traditionally, what we call them.

275
00:25:44,941 --> 00:25:50,370
Mark Russinovich: You know, the beauty of agents is that they can make workflows dynamically.

276
00:25:50,370 --> 00:25:56,340
And I think that this is, instead of you having to go and prescribe a workflow and write it out,

277
00:25:56,340 --> 00:26:05,310
you can give AI a problem, and it'll go figure out how to execute that task and come up with a workflow dynamically.

278
00:26:05,310 --> 00:26:13,530
When I look at those systems, if you've got anything where the workflow is going to be repeated many times,

279
00:26:13,530 --> 00:26:20,580
you want to make that deterministic and not rely on an AI to make it up every time you've got the problem.

280
00:26:20,580 --> 00:26:25,830
So I think where things are not well-defined,

281
00:26:25,830 --> 00:26:32,880
where they're very ad hoc, then AI and agents are great for making up the workflows and solving the problem.

282
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:39,240
But for anything where you've got a repetitive process, you should go try to make it deterministic,

283
00:26:39,240 --> 00:26:44,820
because it's going to be much more efficient, much more reliable, and much cheaper.

284
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:46,500
So all of those things are going to be true.

285
00:26:46,500 --> 00:26:50,760
And I think that this is where I see people over-rotate on, oh, I can have an agent go do this thing.

286
00:26:50,760 --> 00:26:54,300
When I'm like, I look at it and go, actually you could just write a simple workflow.

287
00:26:54,300 --> 00:26:56,640
Ion Stoica: You can have the agent synthesize that workflow.

288
00:26:56,640 --> 00:26:57,300
Mark Russinovich: That's right.

289
00:26:57,300 --> 00:26:57,930
That's right, yeah.

290
00:26:57,930 --> 00:27:02,310
That's the other way to do it is have the agents make the workflow, and then the workflow is deterministic.

291
00:27:02,310 --> 00:27:04,440
Ion Stoica: Yep, that's what I mentioned about.

292
00:27:04,440 --> 00:27:08,310
Yeah. So the other thing is that we used to have--

293
00:27:08,310 --> 00:27:14,010
you have training, and you have serving separate deployments and things like that.

294
00:27:14,010 --> 00:27:19,890
But now with this kind of post-training,

295
00:27:19,890 --> 00:27:24,360
starting even earlier on from reinforcement learning with human feedback.

296
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:29,070
And now it's continual learning and things like that.

297
00:27:29,070 --> 00:27:31,710
It seems that kind of it's a blur out there.

298
00:27:31,710 --> 00:27:41,190
So what should the platform look like -- Mark Russinovich: This is the -- multi-hundred billion dollar question, isn't it?

299
00:27:41,190 --> 00:27:45,210
I think because we run into two things.

300
00:27:45,210 --> 00:27:46,500
I mean, you heard about Maia.

301
00:27:46,500 --> 00:27:47,400
Ion Stoica: Yes, of course.

302
00:27:47,400 --> 00:27:49,980
Mark Russinovich: And Maia is optimized for inference.

303
00:27:49,980 --> 00:27:55,260
Then you hear about NVIDIA's chips, which are optimized, for the most part, their main line for training.

304
00:27:55,260 --> 00:28:03,870
And then you mentioned like GRP or DRPO or any of these post-training things that are happening in the loop with it, where you're doing inference

305
00:28:03,870 --> 00:28:06,510
at the same time you're doing the training.

306
00:28:06,510 --> 00:28:17,130
And so whenever you've got a situation where you can optimize for efficiency, obviously, that's going to be what you want

307
00:28:17,130 --> 00:28:21,000
to do, especially at the scales you're at, where you can save literally tens of millions,

308
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:25,350
hundreds of millions of dollars by optimizing for some specific path.

309
00:28:25,350 --> 00:28:32,430
But when you optimize for that specific path and then things change, and they're not a good fit, that can be a big problem.

310
00:28:32,430 --> 00:28:38,850
And so it's this tension now because we haven't even-- like you said, the evolution of these architectures hasn't finished.

311
00:28:38,850 --> 00:28:43,560
And so if you over-optimize for one workload, you could run into trouble.

312
00:28:43,560 --> 00:28:52,410
So the question is, how general should we be and leave money on the table in the short term?

313
00:28:52,410 --> 00:29:02,250
Because in the long term, we're going to be protected against changes in the architecture or in the application requirements versus taking advantage of optimization now

314
00:29:02,250 --> 00:29:04,170
and potentially being bitten by it later.

315
00:29:04,170 --> 00:29:06,540
I think that that's the tension that you see.

316
00:29:06,540 --> 00:29:07,202
Ion Stoica: Absolutely.

317
00:29:07,245 --> 00:29:07,698
Mark Russinovich: Yeah.

318
00:29:08,568 --> 00:29:08,994
Ion Stoica: Yeah.

319
00:29:10,200 --> 00:29:16,101
Mark Russinovich: All right. So, we're down to a couple of minutes left, according to the clock up here.

320
00:29:16,135 --> 00:29:19,200
So I want to thank you for coming and spending time with us at Build.

321
00:29:19,200 --> 00:29:28,410
Your impact on the industry is monumental, and it's gracious of you to come and take time out of your day to share your thoughts on the evolution of AI infrastructure.

322
00:29:28,410 --> 00:29:29,310
Ion Stoica: Yeah, thank you.

323
00:29:29,310 --> 00:29:32,190
And we haven't touched on-- Mark Russinovich: On Confidential computing.

324
00:29:32,190 --> 00:29:33,600
Ion Stoica: On confidential computing.

325
00:29:33,600 --> 00:29:38,220
So before going, I know I know that this is a topic dear to your heart, Mark.

326
00:29:38,220 --> 00:29:42,107
So can you say something about like security governance and so forth?

327
00:29:42,140 --> 00:29:47,340
Mark Russinovich: Well, absolutely. I mean, they're all -- from security governance, we didn't really get time to deep dive on this.

328
00:29:47,340 --> 00:29:51,690
But when it comes to jailbreaks and prompt injections, I've been doing a lot of research on this.

329
00:29:51,690 --> 00:29:56,970
This is, like I mentioned earlier, a big topic of evolution where we haven't bottomed out yet.

330
00:29:56,970 --> 00:30:03,030
I think I view it as kind of the early days when people were downloading stuff from the internet and just your fingers were crossed.

331
00:30:03,030 --> 00:30:11,940
This is what happens now when you give agents access to your company's private data or your private data or your access tokens is you're kind of crossing your fingers

332
00:30:11,940 --> 00:30:15,900
and hoping that things don't go off the rails and you end up with a problem.

333
00:30:15,900 --> 00:30:21,420
And we've seen some high-profile examples just in the last few months of people getting burned by prompt injections.

334
00:30:21,420 --> 00:30:26,250
So I don't think we've come to the reckoning yet on that and people understanding the risk.

335
00:30:26,250 --> 00:30:31,800
And then with confidential computing, that is another layer of-- the clock just reset to five minutes.

336
00:30:31,800 --> 00:30:33,020
So we've got another five minutes.

337
00:30:34,710 --> 00:30:36,340
Ion Stoica: So now you can, please.

338
00:30:37,406 --> 00:30:40,798
Mark Russinovich: I think backstage they liked the answer so much they wanted me to continue.

339
00:30:43,500 --> 00:30:51,060
So I can talk a little bit about confidential computing now, which is it's independent of AI, of course, but related in some ways.

340
00:30:51,060 --> 00:30:56,760
Because if you think about the sensitivity of the data that people are processing with agentic systems,

341
00:30:56,760 --> 00:31:02,730
if it's in a personal context, you're saying, "Hey, I really want you to help me with my medical advice.

342
00:31:02,730 --> 00:31:05,550
I want you to help me with my financials."

343
00:31:05,550 --> 00:31:12,000
You're giving these agents so much very personal and very sensitive data about yourself.

344
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:21,780
And if you're in an enterprise context, the way you get the value out of these models is giving them access to the sensitive data of your enterprise.

345
00:31:21,780 --> 00:31:28,410
And especially when you're hosting this in a cloud platform or some hoster that's out of your control,

346
00:31:28,410 --> 00:31:34,530
or even in your own environment, you want to minimize the trusted computing base, the code that has access to that data,

347
00:31:34,530 --> 00:31:41,400
to the smallest amount possible where you can have the highest assurance that it's going to behave properly and where bad actors can't get to it.

348
00:31:41,434 --> 00:31:44,954
And confidential computing is -- now we've got another. . .

349
00:31:46,170 --> 00:31:46,830
It reset again.

350
00:31:46,830 --> 00:31:47,280
I was wondering.

351
00:31:47,280 --> 00:31:49,320
I was like, wow, we burned through that time really fast.

352
00:31:49,320 --> 00:31:51,520
So now we have 13 minutes left.

353
00:31:54,610 --> 00:31:55,600
So let me just wrap this up.

354
00:31:55,600 --> 00:31:58,810
And I think there were some more topics that we can discuss.

355
00:31:58,810 --> 00:32:01,180
And maybe we can take some questions from the audience.

356
00:32:01,180 --> 00:32:09,690
But confidential computing, for those of you that aren't familiar with it, it's hardware-based protection of data while it's in use.

357
00:32:09,690 --> 00:32:19,080
So the hardware itself is encrypting memory and creating a privileged boundary around the data such that nothing else on the system can get access

358
00:32:19,080 --> 00:32:22,650
to what's inside of that enclave, they're called.

359
00:32:22,650 --> 00:32:26,370
And it comes with a cool property the hardware provides, which is an attestation,

360
00:32:26,370 --> 00:32:35,670
where it can measure what's inside the enclave and then the application in that enclave can offer that attestation to another system that then can look at it

361
00:32:35,670 --> 00:32:41,970
and say, oh, your compute inside of an enclave, I trust that enclave, I trust your configuration,

362
00:32:41,970 --> 00:32:48,030
I'm going to give you a secret, typically a key, which allows you to go and process that data in the clear.

363
00:32:48,030 --> 00:32:55,772
And it's protected against so many of the threats that not having this enclave protecting that boundary against. And when it comes to agents,

364
00:32:55,806 --> 00:32:59,060
having an attestation of what is the agent?

365
00:32:59,094 --> 00:33:00,975
What is its provenance.?

366
00:33:01,009 --> 00:33:06,780
Do I know that the data that I'm giving to the agent is protected with the highest assurance as possible inside that enclave?

367
00:33:06,780 --> 00:33:13,050
You're going to see a trend with AI and confidential computing that I think is going to really drive forward confidential computing.

368
00:33:13,050 --> 00:33:22,530
Confidential GPUs from NVIDIA, for example, confidential GPUs from AMD, and even Microsoft's Maia processor.

369
00:33:22,530 --> 00:33:25,920
Ion Stoica: Yeah, that's fascinating.

370
00:33:25,920 --> 00:33:31,180
Mark Russinovich: Since we have more time on the clock now, which is kind of nice.

371
00:33:36,090 --> 00:33:40,180
Let me go back to some questions that we skipped earlier for you.

372
00:33:44,730 --> 00:33:48,450
And I guess this is something we really haven't talked about is developer experience.

373
00:33:48,450 --> 00:33:53,430
We've been talking a lot about infrastructure, but you've got all this complexity.

374
00:33:53,430 --> 00:33:57,330
When it comes to developer experience, there's so many options out there.

375
00:33:57,330 --> 00:34:08,250
There's GitHub Copilot CLI there's the SDK, there's OpenClaw, like what -- and there's doing your own loop, calling an LLM directly.

376
00:34:08,250 --> 00:34:11,520
We saw Simon Willison doing this with his own application.

377
00:34:11,520 --> 00:34:14,100
He didn't use any harness.

378
00:34:14,100 --> 00:34:16,050
What's the best approach?

379
00:34:16,050 --> 00:34:21,000
When do you decide, when do you use one harness versus a custom orchestrator?

380
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:25,020
How do you code this, and how do you measure what's the best answer?

381
00:34:25,020 --> 00:34:28,620
And in fact, I wrestle with this too when I'm approaching code.

382
00:34:28,620 --> 00:34:29,460
Ion Stoica: And me as well.

383
00:34:29,460 --> 00:34:34,480
But let me just give. . .

384
00:34:34,513 --> 00:34:39,291
So, I think that. . .

385
00:34:39,325 --> 00:34:48,570
Look, my TLDR is that now we have, again, very powerful coding agents and so forth.

386
00:34:48,570 --> 00:34:53,508
And many of you, I'm sure -- how many of you use coding agents?

387
00:34:53,542 --> 00:34:55,840
Everyone, right?

388
00:34:57,554 --> 00:35:08,157
I do think that -- actually, the biggest challenge is that -- and this is -- it's about how do you know that. . .

389
00:35:08,190 --> 00:35:16,187
the code which is generated is going to work correctly for some kind of definition of correctness, right?

390
00:35:16,221 --> 00:35:20,640
Is doing what you hope you want to do, right?

391
00:35:20,640 --> 00:35:23,097
I think that's the key, right?

392
00:35:23,131 --> 00:35:30,257
And the more code it generates, the harder it is to do that, right? Because --

393
00:35:30,310 --> 00:35:38,710
-- and I think this was, you know, this is also, was true before, right?

394
00:35:38,744 --> 00:35:43,590
Everyone here probably developed a production -- -- system, right?

395
00:35:43,590 --> 00:35:46,320
Or, has been involved in developing a production system.

396
00:35:46,320 --> 00:35:48,540
Where do you spend most of the time?

397
00:35:48,540 --> 00:35:54,603
You don't spending the time or most of the time in prototyping or writing the code; you do spend the time in, kind of,

398
00:35:54,636 --> 00:35:59,220
even, you know, in debugging, then maintaining the code.

399
00:35:59,220 --> 00:36:01,800
It's easily 10 to 1 effort.

400
00:36:01,800 --> 00:36:09,390
So, in some sense, what these coding agents are doing are going to commoditize that kind of writing the code.

401
00:36:09,390 --> 00:36:12,000
But the bottleneck will be more and more in, kind of, verifying.

402
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:20,400
And, yes, you can use these agents to say, "Hey you-- ", you know,"check that, is that correct?"

403
00:36:20,400 --> 00:36:21,660
But it's very hard, you know?

404
00:36:21,660 --> 00:36:22,885
Some agents are lazy.

405
00:36:22,918 --> 00:36:29,040
So, without you understanding the code, at least at this point today, I don't think --

406
00:36:29,040 --> 00:36:38,790
and having continuously having testing it and enhancing the test, I don't think you can trust that code that is doing what it's supposed to do.

407
00:36:38,790 --> 00:36:44,910
Because that kind of reward hacking is very different from what you can think about.

408
00:36:44,910 --> 00:36:46,350
Let me give you just one example.

409
00:36:46,350 --> 00:36:49,650
I have so many examples about that, but I'm giving you the simplest one.

410
00:36:49,650 --> 00:36:51,810
You mentioned earlier about load balancing.

411
00:36:51,810 --> 00:37:01,380
So, say you are going to say, okay, I want to develop a load balancer algorithms and, you know, for and -- you know, using the agents.

412
00:37:01,380 --> 00:37:07,590
And we did that, we have that a few months ago using EPLB, right, Expert Parallelism Load Balancer, of course.

413
00:37:07,590 --> 00:37:15,393
And then what you want to do is the metrics, you want this, the load you are going to handle.

414
00:37:15,427 --> 00:37:17,520
You know, you want to maximize that load.

415
00:37:17,520 --> 00:37:24,289
Now, one solution is simply, which we've seen, that because that's a metric --

416
00:37:24,322 --> 00:37:29,310
the "hill climbs", the load, is just dropping requests.

417
00:37:29,310 --> 00:37:38,940
Right? Now, you wouldn't think that when you tell someone, even when you write the PRD about the load balancers, "Hey, you shouldn't drop the request."

418
00:37:38,940 --> 00:37:41,130
It's that's kind of understandable, right?

419
00:37:41,130 --> 00:37:48,270
But these models, they are going to do that because they are going to maximize the things you told them to maximize, right?

420
00:37:48,270 --> 00:37:50,910
In the simplest and the easiest way.

421
00:37:50,910 --> 00:37:52,830
So that's kind of why I'm saying it, right?

422
00:37:52,830 --> 00:37:56,970
And there are many things which you can pass all the unit tests and so forth.

423
00:37:56,970 --> 00:38:06,840
And then when you look deeper, there are things which are, in a way, overfit to your test you couldn't imagine, right?

424
00:38:06,840 --> 00:38:10,770
It's like that's kind of the things you still need to, I think, at this stage.

425
00:38:10,770 --> 00:38:17,130
I don't know what will happen in a few months, in one year, but right now, you have to be able to specify.

426
00:38:17,130 --> 00:38:24,390
If you didn't specify something, you can almost guarantee that the systems will find something around it.

427
00:38:24,390 --> 00:38:27,510
And there are three places a specification comes from.

428
00:38:27,510 --> 00:38:32,190
So one is, basically -- Actually, there are only two.

429
00:38:32,190 --> 00:38:33,295
And you know, like. . .

430
00:38:33,328 --> 00:38:35,880
One is that the training data set.

431
00:38:35,880 --> 00:38:39,990
The training data set, you kind of train, and some specifications come from here.

432
00:38:39,990 --> 00:38:44,940
Like, for instance, one year ago, when you generated code, you have a lot of vulnerabilities.

433
00:38:44,940 --> 00:38:49,020
Now, you have fewer vulnerabilities when you generate code with agentic.

434
00:38:49,020 --> 00:38:51,540
Right? And the other one is from the prompt.

435
00:38:51,540 --> 00:38:54,330
You have to specify what it should do or not.

436
00:38:54,330 --> 00:39:02,040
If it's not in the prompt or it's not in the training data set, it's going to find a way around.

437
00:39:02,040 --> 00:39:02,970
Mark Russinovich: It'll fill in the blanks.

438
00:39:02,970 --> 00:39:06,450
Ion Stoica: Yeah, so that's kind of, I think, that's, I think, the main bottleneck.

439
00:39:07,076 --> 00:39:09,969
Mark Russinovich: All right. So, now we really have six minutes, and I'll give you a question that I --

440
00:39:10,002 --> 00:39:16,500
that I think is the -- the question that I get a lot, and I've got my own opinion about this, but I'm curious about yours.

441
00:39:16,500 --> 00:39:17,640
Ion Stoica: So you'll answer it after I answer it.

442
00:39:17,640 --> 00:39:19,760
Mark Russinovich: Yeah, I'll answer it.

443
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:27,000
And this is, I talk to people, and I explain I've got so many war stories of AI going off the rails.

444
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:30,480
I mean, me and Scott, we have got this podcast.

445
00:39:30,480 --> 00:39:33,870
I talk about how I basically sculpt, which I always have.

446
00:39:33,870 --> 00:39:36,060
But even with AI, I'm watching what it's doing.

447
00:39:36,060 --> 00:39:38,820
I'm control seeing it.

448
00:39:38,820 --> 00:39:41,460
I'm telling it, "That's not what I want you to do."

449
00:39:41,460 --> 00:39:50,030
And I see it creating this test or going off in some direction, filling in the blanks with something that is ridiculous or that I don't want.

450
00:39:50,030 --> 00:39:52,130
And I tell people this and I say,

451
00:39:52,130 --> 00:40:01,040
you just can't give it a prompt or a PRD and expect that you're going to get a system that works reliably and efficiently and does all the things

452
00:40:01,040 --> 00:40:03,620
that you imagine you would want it to do.

453
00:40:03,620 --> 00:40:12,840
And even if you go and try to specify it, you cannot specify it deeply enough to guarantee that it's not going to do things that you don't want.

454
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:24,816
A lot of what we do is based off of kind of environmental context and just kind of the taste that we've developed.

455
00:40:24,850 --> 00:40:33,780
And so I'm like-- I just don't see a path to this world where You don't need engineers that understand what's going on and don't know how to guide the system when it goes off the rails.

456
00:40:33,780 --> 00:40:43,680
And then, I get this pushback of, "No, Mark, you're just--" You know? That's a bet that I would take with you every day because it's going to be solved.

457
00:40:43,680 --> 00:40:51,420
Maybe two years from now, maybe five years from now, maybe 10 years from now, the AI will be so good that you won't need that.

458
00:40:51,420 --> 00:40:54,810
Your example where you said the load balancer was dropping requests.

459
00:40:54,810 --> 00:41:00,870
What if you have an adversarial code reviewer go look at the system and say, "You know what, you shouldn't be dropping a request."

460
00:41:00,870 --> 00:41:05,970
And it's, is this system well-behaved? It'll say, "No, it's not." And so you just address that.

461
00:41:05,970 --> 00:41:07,770
So where do you lie on this?

462
00:41:07,770 --> 00:41:14,850
Do you think that-- would you be bold enough to say it's never going to be solved or do you think we'll get to the point where we'll have the ability

463
00:41:14,850 --> 00:41:21,270
to give a specification, it'll come out great, and you won't need a human?

464
00:41:21,270 --> 00:41:24,340
Ion Stoica: I think that in theoretical--

465
00:41:27,450 --> 00:41:34,380
from the theoretical perspective, it's very hard for me to see how it's going to be ever solved.

466
00:41:34,380 --> 00:41:44,010
And fundamentally, because we know from Gödel that no axiomatic system can be complete.

467
00:41:44,010 --> 00:41:44,580
Mark Russinovich: Yeah.

468
00:41:44,580 --> 00:41:49,560
Ion Stoica: So in some sense, that kind of tells you something.

469
00:41:49,560 --> 00:41:58,490
I think that when we look at other techniques to do it, like for instance, assume that you have a formal specification.

470
00:41:58,490 --> 00:42:02,450
You can write in Lean, on Coal, and so forth.

471
00:42:02,450 --> 00:42:13,650
So from that one, if I have the formal specification, then you can generate the code, and then you can generate the proof that the code satisfies the specification.

472
00:42:13,650 --> 00:42:18,360
So that kind of is good, and you can see that.

473
00:42:18,360 --> 00:42:25,590
The problem is that obviously then you need to write that specification, which is kind of not easy.

474
00:42:25,590 --> 00:42:34,230
And even that when you write the specification, it cannot -- you know, it's like, if it's incomplete, right?

475
00:42:34,230 --> 00:42:37,080
You don't specify a property because, you know, like, in this case,

476
00:42:37,080 --> 00:42:43,740
I write a specification for load balancers, but I don't write, "Oh, you shouldn't drop the packets," right?

477
00:42:43,740 --> 00:42:50,070
So maybe if there is a conservation law there, you can argue that you should put it in.

478
00:42:50,070 --> 00:42:51,930
But there can be assumptions, right?

479
00:42:51,930 --> 00:42:56,820
Because any specification, anything, you have to have some assumptions, right, to make the proof.

480
00:42:56,820 --> 00:43:00,600
But you are not going to imagine all of the assumptions, right?

481
00:43:00,600 --> 00:43:02,610
Mark Russinovich: I got to -- you know what I think?

482
00:43:02,610 --> 00:43:05,250
You know what is a full specification?

483
00:43:05,250 --> 00:43:07,605
Actually, programming languages.

484
00:43:07,639 --> 00:43:10,170
Ion Stoica: Almost, almost. Even that is not.

485
00:43:10,170 --> 00:43:12,420
Let me give you an example -- even that is not.

486
00:43:12,420 --> 00:43:15,073
Mark Russinovich: Yeah, that's true. But that's -- Ion Stoica: Like, I remember, you know,

487
00:43:15,106 --> 00:43:19,020
we have this kind of, I'm not sure really, like, this peer-to-peer court system and so forth.

488
00:43:19,020 --> 00:43:22,770
I was young faculty there, still implementing all of these things.

489
00:43:22,770 --> 00:43:25,920
And really, really, I thought it's bulletproof.

490
00:43:25,920 --> 00:43:32,330
And we are testing it on this -- I don't remember, how many remember about Planet Labs.

491
00:43:32,330 --> 00:43:37,520
Twenty years ago, this Planet Lab, which is kind of an infrastructure, internet infrastructure,

492
00:43:37,520 --> 00:43:45,390
or each organization have these servers, and then you deploy at the servers applications, and then you communicate.

493
00:43:45,390 --> 00:43:46,620
And it breaks.

494
00:43:46,620 --> 00:43:47,640
And I don't know how it breaks.

495
00:43:47,640 --> 00:43:48,960
It has to work.

496
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:57,330
And when we look about, it was an assumption we missed, and the assumption we've made, implicitly, is a kind of transient connectivity.

497
00:43:57,330 --> 00:43:59,970
If A communicates with B and B communicates with C,

498
00:43:59,970 --> 00:44:08,040
then A should communicate with C. And in the Internet as a whole, right, because it's not behind NATs and so forth, this has to hold.

499
00:44:08,040 --> 00:44:14,610
And it should have hold, except that is one university which misconfigured their servers.

500
00:44:14,610 --> 00:44:18,180
So that blocks some communication, some traffic.

501
00:44:18,180 --> 00:44:20,160
So that's kind of, I always think about that.

502
00:44:20,160 --> 00:44:20,580
Mark Russinovich: Yeah, good example.

503
00:44:20,580 --> 00:44:27,480
Ion Stoica: I couldn't, you know, it's like, I was actually, we were correct about the assumptions.

504
00:44:27,480 --> 00:44:33,480
It should have held, but it didn't because of a mistake in the configuration in the platform.

505
00:44:33,480 --> 00:44:35,190
So that's kind of what I'm trying to say.

506
00:44:35,190 --> 00:44:36,240
It's extremely hard.

507
00:44:36,240 --> 00:44:39,870
Mark Russinovich: Yeah. So, Now we are really, I think, at time.

508
00:44:39,870 --> 00:44:42,930
I haven't seen the clock jump up again.

509
00:44:42,930 --> 00:44:45,780
But it's been a fun conversation, a really fun conversation.

510
00:44:45,780 --> 00:44:48,540
And if you like this, this is the experiment.

511
00:44:48,540 --> 00:44:49,830
It's the first time we've done something like this.

512
00:44:49,830 --> 00:44:51,720
If you like this, let us know.

513
00:44:51,720 --> 00:44:53,370
So we'll try to do it again.

514
00:44:53,370 --> 00:44:56,730
If you find it boring, also let us know.

515
00:44:56,730 --> 00:45:03,120
And I think the people that thought it was boring probably already left, so I think-- Ion Stoica: We have a biased audience here.

516
00:45:03,120 --> 00:45:05,220
Mark Russinovich: But thanks very much for coming to Build,

517
00:45:05,220 --> 00:45:10,106
and I hope to see you tomorrow morning in my Azure Innovation Talk, where I'll talk a little bit about some of these things.

518
00:45:10,786 --> 00:45:11,426
Ion Stoica: Thank you very much.
